ntially the unexpected  happens and all we can really do is  you know do our best to be resilient so  we try to build resilient systems such  that it can handle even  unexpected issues so start with server  failure  pretty typical way to handle silver  failure is load balancing this is in  this case we're no different  we build our own load balancers though  so that is actually pretty different  in general at braintree we just really  we like components that are  simple and easy to understand so we tend  to shy away from  black boxes and so load bouncers are a  giant kind of black  black box appliance um you know they  work well for some people  they also people love to complain about  them i mean if you you hear lots of  horror stories about like f5s and stuff  like that  so we prefer just to kind of build our  own we understand it at every level we  can tune it for availability for  security we can tweak it to our needs  so we just really like to control it so  what we use is we use  lvs or ipvs this is the linux virtual  server project or ipv virtual server  it's basically kernel level routing so  our load balancers are just linux  servers  traffic comes into them and then via  kernel level rules the traffic gets  routed to other backends  so it's pretty simple we manage a  cluster of them with pacemaker so it  handles failover if a load balancer dies  and then we have two components that we  wrote called big brother and limits  paper and i'll go into these in a moment  so this is you know the the diagram of  what it looks like so traffic comes in  either externally or internally we load  bounce a lot of internal services  it goes to a virtual ip living on one  load bouncer um each load balancer has  many ips for all the different services  and then the load balancer forwards the  traffic to back end in this case apaches  and the piece to note is that big  brother runs in the load balancer and  litmus paper runs on the back end  so big brother it's a ruby apple road it  like i said runs in load bouncers its  job is just to check the status of the  back ends  so every second it's just pulling back  end servers getting you know the status  of how they're doing  and then it updates ipvs rules so if it  discovers the back end's down or not  responding it updates the kernel level  rules to change the routing such that  new traffic won't flow  won't flow there and so it's just doing  that constantly it's just in a big loop  and big brother's open source you can  check it out on our github  so on the other side is litmus paper  limits paper is also ruby app  it's the piece that big brother is  querying via http  and the idea is with with limits paper  is it's a consistent interface that big  brother can query  so it runs on all of our back ends and  it returns a health level and  its job is to kind of map between  whatever it needs to do  to that health level so for an apache  server it probably just turns around and  curls apache  and if it patches up then it says i'm up  and then it combines that with  you know system level info so if the  load is high but apache is up maybe the  health isn't 100 it's  80. if it's totally down the health is  zero that kind of stuff so  limits paper just figures out what it  needs to do for that server for that  type and it returns a health level to  big brother  and this is also open source you can  check it out so there's the diagram  again  so like i said big brother is just  querying the back ends litmus papers  responding with the healths  and then based on big brother rules the  the load balancer will forward or not  forward traffic to those back ends  so it's pretty it's pretty  straightforward okay so that works  really well for stateless services  things like apache where you can send  traffic to three different apaches and  then if one you know goes down you just  send it to two apaches instead  we also have a class of state full  servers where you can't just send  all of your traffic to all of them so  load balancers are one of them you know  the virtual ip lives on one load  balancer  postgres is another big one so we have  you know primaries and standbys you  can't send all of your  traffic to all of your postcard servers  you need the rights to go to the  primaries  and just try to send right to the  standbys you'll get errors so for these  we use pacemaker to manage failover  so pacemaker just kind of manages a  cluster of things so it manages a  cluster of our postgres  it keeps track of which one's primary  which one's standby if the primary fails  pacemaker will do the failover for us  and they'll promote the standby  and then we just move virtual ips so the  app  just knows the virtual ip of like the  database the virtual ip of search the  virtual pa pretty much everything  and then when things die either the  unbounce or pacemaker will  move them and then the virtual ip just  moves along with it so we don't need to  reconfigure anything when things die  okay so that's server failure now we  have network failure  so fun site to look at internetpulse.com  it shows you  basically outages between different isps  internet service providers across the  internet  so i took this screenshot last week it  turns out that issues in isps are  actually very common  so you can see like um at the top you  can see the red box is between  centurylink and savis  so if you're running a service on status  and you're trying to connect to it from  your home which is centurylink let's say  you might have trouble connecting you  can see like there's  there's a lot of isp issues one of the  things i learned at braintree is that  it's like network issues are actually  very common it's amazing sometimes if  the internet actually works  because there are so many issues just  with connections across the wire  so pretty much if we see connection  issues we'll pull up this site and you  can kind of  we know which isps we serve out of which  data centers and you can kind of look at  the different connection issues with  other isps  okay so we have two kinds of networking  i want to talk about here so the first  one is inbound this is  a merchant wants to charge a credit card  so they make an api call to us so that's  inbound coming to us so for that we use  bgp  to route traffic through multiple isps  and data centers  so bgp is the border gateway protocol  it's essentially the protocol of the  internet  so it you know use dns to go from a name  to an ip address  so bgp is how you figure out where to go  with that ip address  so our routers participate in bgp they  broadcast  you know the ips that we handle and then  bgp handles like finding the best path  through the internet to get to us so if  an isp goes down  bgp will essentially unbalance it and  then it'll find another route to us  so in general bgp does shortest route  with some caveats  but if the route is down it'll find a  new route that's you know the one that's  up  so by participating in this you know we  have multiple paths coming into us  so depending on where the merchant is  where the customer is  the traffic will route to us via  different isps and different data  centers  and then we use monitoring services we  use pingdom pretty heavily and we use  our own kind of servers that we  scattered throughout the world  and we basically just do that to monitor  it so bgp is great it unbalances it it  works  but it's not always instantaneous we've  seen issues where especially when isp  outages are partial  we've seen issues where we see a lot of  problems coming in via one route  but bgb hasn't figured it out yet or  hasn't fully unbalanced  so since we have our own monitoring we  can kind of detect these cases and then  we just manually yank the bgp routes  and force the traffic over a different  path  so that's kind of how we so bcp  generally does its own thing it works  but we also  can do it manually if we need to based  on our own monitoring  okay so that's trouble coming to us we  also have the problem we  we are not the end point for most of  this traffic we need to go out  across the network to actually tell  whether their credit cards are valid or  whether they have enough money on that  so in general we don't we don't talk  about visa directly we talk to  what are called like merchant fires of  costing networks in general  so for people that know payments we  talked about like chase payment attack  thesis versus data  uh we talked to international talk a lot  of things directly  because we need essentially we need to  get back to whoever whichever issue bank  issues  to know if you have money on my credit  card so  if like if i leave here with a ton of  these coffee numbers but we need to  basically make outbound calls to them  so you may call to us we need to turn  around and make another call to figure  out  how much is available on this card and  so like i said the isp outages are very  are usually partial  it's rare that you have a total outage  it does happen i think last year there  was a construction in chicago and they  just  drilled right to the line like one of  the big issues in chicago and took out  the entire thing  but generally like a router fails  somewhere or there's some packet loss  so they're not usually full outages so  we've gotten into cases where  we have multiple isps but we can't reach  every endpoint we have to hit  on all of our xps so it's not like we  can say like oh we're having problems  with this one let's just take it out  we'll use the other one  maybe they're both having partial  outages and we can only reach some on  one and some on the other  and so we really wanted was a way to  choose an isp  for the end point we're trying to get to  so some are better than others for  certain paths we want to use the best  one for that path  and so we built our processor proxies  that's what we call them so instead of  the dispatcher connecting directly we  connect through a proxy  we run one proxy per endpoint so per  processor like we have one one for chase  let's say  pinned to a specific isp and then we can  load balance over them so if there are  issues we can we  allow our load balancing to take them  out  and this allows us to route around  issues on isps that are  partial essentially so the piece we  wrote to this is called mallory  it's also pilot tornado for the same  reasons it's just shoveling traffic back  and forth we find something very  lightweight  it proxies requests and this is a big  one so we looked around you know we  didn't essentially we didn't  want to write our own proxy but we kind  of looked around what was out there  and you know ssl verification is a big  it's kind of a big deal so ssl does  encryption  but it also does verification which is  you want to know that the end point  you're connected to is actually who you  think it is so most of the processing  networks use ssl  um and a lot of proxies will will do ssl  encryption but they don't have to do  verification so they'll ensure that your  traffic's encrypted  but not that you're connecting to who  you think you're connecting to and so  one of the things we do is  we strongly you know verify the sl  certificate so we can't get man in the  middle  so if somehow we were to get man in the  middle of upstream our request would  fail rather than send credit card data  to the wrong person  um and then we would we would notice  that our requests are failing we would  look into it  so it's really it's really unfortunate  that if you look online and you look at  you kind of google this  a lot of recommendations online are oh  just turn off sl encryption right curl  dash k like just turn it off  but i mean ssl is very powerful and you  should definitely use it  as much as possible even with  verification  so the other cool thing with mallory  another reason we wrote it is it acts  like limits paper  so the same way big brother is querying  litmus paper big brother can directly  query mallory  so mallory is doing the proxing and it's  keeping track of how that proxy is going  and then if the last few requests  haven't gone so well when big brother  queries it you can say like actually  proxy's not going that well i'm gonna  return to low health or zero health  saying that this route is just not good  right now  and mallory is also open source you can  check it out so this is kind of what the  diagram looks like  so the dispatcher is trying to make a  call to the processor on the right  it you know makes a call through a  virtual ip that's load balanced over two  mallories each mallory is pinned to an  isp  in that data center and then the traffic  will eventually route there so if  the isp1 goes down the top mallet will  unbalance and traffic will flow through  the bottom mallory  and this you know this works when we  look at the logs we see mallory's kind  of unbalancing periodically of today  like  little issues will happen but we  generally survive them without without  really noticing  so connection fails we do get connection  failures right  the way we know that a route is bad is  we will get a connection failure trying  to talk  over it so this is something we do have  to deal with our general  our general practice is we just let  things heal so in the case of mallory  we get a connection failure we know that  when big brother goes to query it will  unbalance it so we just let the system  heal  with things like the database we know  that pacemaker will handle the failover  so we just wait like we just wait for it  to handle the issue  and then we retry and we do retrying at  a lot of different levels of our stack  so in the case of processor calls we'll  often do several  two or three if we can't connect knowing  that if there's an issue mallory will  unbalance and then we'll reroute around  it  uh with things like the database if our  database our primary database were to  crash  we would we would lose the request in  flight there's you know  that's not what we can do about that but  before our dispatchers pick up another  request from redis  they do heartbeats and so they won't  pick up a request until they can  actually check that the whole app is  working again  and that means that the database has to  fully fail over the noon has to come up  then the heartbeat to start working and  then the dispatchers will pick up the  requests again  and so this is the way we kind of retry  there so we we won't pick up requests if  we know that things are busted we'll  wait until things have healed and then  start processing again  okay so just to summarize kind of the  unplanned failures load balancing we do  for  server failure and also kind of  components of network failure  we have redundancy across isps inbound  we use bgp  for routing outbound we use processor  proxies to kind of target the right isp  for the right endpoint  and then in general we just let things  heal and then try again and we do like i  said we do retrying at many different  levels of our stack  okay so the last section people make  mistakes  um it's it's inevitable right people can  cause outages you run the wrong command  you do the wrong thing  you cause an outage people can also make  things worse  you know let's say there's already an  outage and you just take a long time  fixing it or you run a command to try to  figure out what's going on and you just  make the outage worse  so you know people people can be a  problem  so here's a couple examples the big ec2  outage of 2011  they say that the traffic shift was  executed incorrectly so you know that  someone there just you know probably ran  the wrong set of commands  github outage also a few years ago  this one due to configuration error they  accidentally dropped their production  database rather than their test database  and had to then go restore their  production database from a backup and i  believe they were down for a good chunk  of that time  so that one you know they