ing in multiple environments you  know maybe you're testing something out  in tests but then running it in  production you're switching back and  forth  you know you want to know which ones are  in tests and which ones are in  production and we just put that right in  the prompt  we also have read-only databases and  logs so if you're you know interrogating  somebody trying to figure something out  you don't have to worry about  accidentally running a bad query you  know one year read-only you can't  destroy anything and two you're doing  read-only on a database that's not even  in the main you know database  in the data center so even if you were  to run a query that didn't modify  anything but was super slow and brought  down the database so you don't have to  worry about it  same thing with logs we you know you're  we look at logs on log servers that are  shipped out of our main  center so if you were to run a really  expensive graph and slow down the box  like you don't have to worry about  actually slowing down production  at that so basically just try to shield  you as much as possible from the actual  running software  and then the other thing is monitoring  so we use nagios pretty extensively  so we use so nagios monitors everything  from like the load on a box  uh uptime to we write a lot of custom  checks like did the nightly settlement  job finished successfully  um did we bill all the subscriptions  that we're supposed to build did we miss  any things like that we have a lot of  checks just at all levels we have that  hooked up to pagerduty so it'll call you  so a check fails it calls you so if  you're working in production you  generally have nagios open  and you have page duty set up to you so  if you do something  and something goes wrong you immediately  get a phone call and you know that you  did something and you're right there  ready to fix it  and then we also have this multi-app log  tail so our general way we use tmux we  split vertically  you 